# Why do governments fail even when they mean well?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Hayek's knowledge problem: can the state know what the market knows? Information failures versus incentive failures"
next: {"title":"Why do governments fail when politicians follow their incentives?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/56-why-do-governments-fail-when-politicians.cast"}

## The perfect forest
setting: {"ground":"grass"}
sources:
    - id: scott98
      title: Seeing Like a State
      authors: James C. Scott
      year: 1998
      finding: German scientific forestry (from c. 1765–1800) replaced mixed woods with Norway spruce; the first rotation (about 80 years) was a success, but a forester he quotes (Plochmann) puts the drop over two or three generations of pure spruce at one or two site classes, 'a production loss of 20 to 30 percent'. Clearing underbrush, deadfalls and snags cut the insects, birds and fungi that build soil.

