    card title "The perfect forest"

Why do governments fail even when the people running them mean well, know their trade, and take no bribes?
    draw mixed

Start in the German state forests around 1800. The foresters there had one g (@draw t_goal@)oal: a steady flow of timber for the treasury, year after year.
    draw t_place

B: And they were the best-trained foresters anywhere. By 1900, forestry schools  (@highlight t_school effect underline@)from France to America were teaching their methods.
    draw t_school

They measured every tree, cleared the tangled mixed woods, and plan (@draw row1 row2 row3 parallel true@)ted neat rows of one fast-growing tree: No (@draw t_spruce@)rway spruce.
    erase mixed t_school

The first crop, ready after about eighty years, was  (@highlight row1 row2 row3 effect pulse@)a triumph: more wood, more uniform wood, more money.
    draw t_crop1

B: So they planted again. Same rows, same tree, same experts. I'd guess  (@highlight t_crop2 effect underline@)it went even better the second time; they'd had practice.
    draw t_crop2

    quiz "What happened to the yield after two or three generations of pure spruce?" judge false pool false
        right: It fell, and not by a little.
        * It rose
        * About the same
        * It fell

    erase t_crop2

It fell. A German forester later put the loss over two o (@fade row2 row3 to 0.35@)r three generations of pure spruce at 20 to 30 percent.
    draw stamp t_yield

B: From the best foresters in the world? What went wrong?
    highlight stamp effect box

Tidiness, partly. T (@draw x1@)hey cleared the unde (@draw x2@)rbrush, the fallen w (@draw x3@)ood and the dead standing trees, and with them the (@draw x4@) insects, birds and fungi that build the soil.
    erase t_goal t_crop1

Nobody stole anything. Ho (@draw t_tables@)w a forest feeds itself simply wasn't in their tables, and ecologists still don't fully understand it.

    erase t_tables

B: So that's the puzzle: a failure with no villain in it.
    text t_villain "no villain" #8a5fa8 x 820 y 200 font_size 42

## Wanting or knowing?
sources:
    - id: hayek45
      title: The Use of Knowledge in Society
      authors: Friedrich A. Hayek
      year: 1945
      finding: The knowledge an economy uses never exists in concentrated form, but as dispersed bits held by separate individuals; the 'knowledge of the particular circumstances of time and place'.
      doi: 10.1257/aer.35.4.519

    path g_v #3d3833 points [[460,170],[460,600]] hidden true
    path g_h #3d3833 points [[190,385],[730,385]] hidden true
    shape g_box #3d3833 shape rect x 460 y 385 width 540 height 430 hidden true
    text h_k "knows enough?" x 460 y 650 font_size 34 hidden true
    text h_ky "yes" #8f887c x 325 y 618 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_kn "no" #8f887c x 595 y 618 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_w "wants the\nright thing?" x 90 y 630 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_wy "yes" #8f887c x 150 y 492.5 font_size 30 hidden true
    text h_wn "no" #8f887c x 150 y 277.5 font_size 30 hidden true
    text c_ok "it works" #5d7f4e x 325 y 512.5 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_both "both" #8f887c x 595 y 297.5 font_size 36 hidden true
    text c_inc "incentive\nfailure" #b5482e x 325 y 307.5 font_size 38 hidden true
    text c_know "knowledge\nfailure" #2f6b8f x 555 y 522.5 font_size 38 hidden true
    icon forest size 70 x 685 y 522.5 icon_look picture of "evergreen tree" hidden true
    text cure_inc "cure: watchdogs,\nbonuses" #b5482e x 325 y 222.5 font_size 28 hidden true
    text cure_know "cure:\nfinding out" #2f6b8f x 595 y 437.5 font_size 28 hidden true
    text bonus "+ bonus\nper m³ of wood" #5d7f4e x 865 y 500 font_size 32 hidden true
    mark bonus_x around bonus kind cross hidden true
    arrow k_ar #2f6b8f from.x 740 from.y 522.5 to.x 790 to.y 585 hidden true
    text k1 "dispersed" #2f6b8f cites ["hayek45"] x 875 y 590 font_size 36 hidden true
    text k2 "tacit" #2f6b8f x 875 y 525 font_size 36 hidden true
    text k3 "changing" #2f6b8f x 875 y 460 font_size 36 hidden true
    text k4 "unknowable" #2f6b8f x 875 y 395 font_size 36 hidden true
    portrait hayek x 875 y 240 width 170 of "Friedrich Hayek" cameo true hidden true

    card title "Wanting or knowing?"

To see how, ask any official two separate qu (@draw h_w h_wy h_wn@)estions: does she want what the public wants, and does she know enough to get it?
    draw g_box g_v g_h h_k h_ky h_kn

B: If she (@highlight h_wn effect circle@) doesn't want it, because of a bribe, a vote to win or a career to protect, that's (@draw c_inc@) an incentive failure.

If she wants it but can't know enough, that's a knowledge failure.  (@draw forest@)The foresters sit right here.
    draw c_know c_ok c_both

    hide g_box g_v g_h h_k h_ky h_kn h_w h_wy h_wn c_inc c_know c_ok c_both forest

B: Your turn. Sort these four into the two kinds of failure.
    cards sortq check each
        incentive failure: Inspector paid to look away
        knowledge failure: Fish quota set on last year's count
        incentive failure: Minister's bridge in his own village
        knowledge failure: New road where the traffic used to be

    ask "Which failed for want of will, and which for want of knowledge? Sort each case." on sortq store srt stage own voice b pool false
        right: All four: the bribe and the village bridge lacked will; the stale quota and the misplaced road lacked knowledge.
        wrong: You got {srt} of {srt.total}. The bribe and the bridge lacked will; the quota and the road lacked knowledge.

    erase sortq

    show g_box g_v g_h h_k h_ky h_kn h_w h_wy h_wn c_inc c_know c_ok c_both forest

The test: would she have done better by wanting harder, or by knowing more?
    highlight c_inc c_know effect underline

B: Then try this on the foresters: pay each (@draw bonus@) of them a bonus for every cubic metre of wood. Does the  (@highlight forest effect pulse@)forest come back?

No. A bonus fixes wanting, not knowing. They'd plant the same rows of spruce, only faster.
    draw bonus_x

    erase bonus bonus_x

Knowledge goes missing in four ways. It can b (@draw k_ar k1@)e dispersed: Hayek's point tha (@draw hayek@)t what an economy runs on is scattered in scraps across millions of heads.

B: It can be  (@draw k2@)tacit: known but never written down, like a  (@highlight k2 effect underline@)fisher's feel for when the weather will turn.
    erase hayek

It can be (@draw k3@) changing: true when the plan was written,  (@highlight k3 effect underline@)stale by the time it's carried out.

B: Or it can (@draw k4@) be unknowable: nobody knows it yet, not even the experts. Th (@highlight k4 forest effect circle@)e forest soil was that.

So the two failures need different cures: watchdo (@draw cure_inc@)gs and bonuses fix wanting; only finding ou (@draw cure_know@)t fixes knowing.

## The wells of Bangladesh
sources:
    - id: smith2000
      title: 'Contamination of drinking-water by arsenic in Bangladesh: a public health emergency'
      authors: Allan H. Smith, Elena O. Lingas, Mahfuzar Rahman
      year: 2000
      finding: '''The largest poisoning of a population in history''; of Bangladesh''s 125 million people, between 35 and 77 million at risk; the millions of tube wells installed to prevent gastrointestinal disease were not tested for arsenic.'
      url: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2560840
    - id: madajewicz2007
      title: Can information alone change behavior? Response to arsenic contamination of groundwater in Bangladesh
      authors: Malgosia Madajewicz, Alexander Pfaff, Alexander van Geen and others
      year: 2007
      finding: Learning that the household's own well is unsafe raises the probability of switching to another well within a year by 0.37 (Araihazar, Bangladesh).

