    card title "The wells of Bangladesh"

The hardest kind is the k (@draw t_bd pond t_pond@)nowledge nobody has. Here's a case of it, from Bangladesh.

B: Pond and river wa (@draw germ t_germ@)ter there spread cholera and diarrhoea, which killed many children.

So from the 1970s the  (@draw ar wells@)government, backed by UNICEF and the World Bank, pu (@draw t_70s@)shed millions of tube wells: groundwater pumped up a pipe, free of germs.

B: But nobody tested tha (@draw t_notest@)t water for arsenic, a poison that seeps naturally from the sediments underground.

In the early 1990s, arseni (@draw t_found@)c turned up in the wells, across huge  (@highlight wells effect box@)parts of the country.
    erase t_notest

B: A paper in the WHO's Bulletin in 2000 called  (@draw big@)it the largest poisoning of a population in history: 35 to 77 million people at risk, out of  (@draw t_of@)125 million.

Well-meant, done competently, and wr (@highlight big effect underline@)ong about something nobody knew to ask. So what helped?
    fade pond germ t_germ ar to 0.3

Not a smarter plan from the capital.  (@draw t_painted@)Testers went from well to well and painted  (@draw d1 d2 d3 parallel true@)each spout: green for saf (@draw d4 d5 d6 parallel true@)e, red for unsafe.
    erase big t_of t_found

    erase t_painted

B: Guess first: when a family learned their own well was red, how much more likely were they to switch within a year?
    draw sc

    ask "Click on the line where you think it is." on sc store g after keep voice b pool false
        right: Close: you said {g}, it was {g.true} points.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was {g.true} points.

Thirty-seven percentage points more likely, in one large study. Many simply walked to a neighbour's green well.
    highlight d2 d4 d5 effect circle

    erase sc

B: So a red s (@draw t_signal@)pout works a bit like a price: one cheap si (@highlight dots effect box@)gnal, put right where the decision is made.

That's the cure (@draw t_cure@) for a knowledge failure: find out, and get what you f (@highlight t_cure effect underline@)ound to the people who choose.

## Whose failure?
sources:
    - id: vangeen2002
      title: Promotion of well-switching to mitigate the current arsenic crisis in Bangladesh
      authors: A. van Geen, H. Ahsan, A. H. Horneman and others
      year: 2002
      finding: 'Survey of about 5,000 wells in Araihazar (2000): 94% privately owned, installed by small local companies.'
      url: https://www.ldeo.columbia.edu/~avangeen/pdf/vanGeen_WHO_02.pdf
    - id: orphanides
      title: The quest for prosperity without inflation
      authors: Athanasios Orphanides
      year: 2003
      finding: Real-time estimates of the US output gap in the 1970s were far more negative than later revised estimates; policy that looked reasonable on real-time data fed inflation.

