    card title "Whose failure?"

B: But was this really a government failure? Most of (@fade grid to 0.35@) those wells weren't the state's.
    draw grid pumps parallel true

Fair point. In one district surveyed in 20 (@draw r94@)00, 94 percent of the wells we (@draw t_dist@)re privately owned, put in by small local firms.

B: So families and the market didn' (@draw t_all@)t know about the arsenic either. The unknowable is unknowable for everyone.

True. The difference is scal (@draw t_scale@)e and feedback: a plan copies one mistake (@draw trees@) everywhere at once, like the same spruce across a country's forests.
    erase r94 t_dist t_all

B: And it was the state that test (@draw t_state@)ed the wells and painted the spouts. Governments find things out too.

And the two failures tangle: a mistake  (@draw ar_t t_tang@)lasts longer when nobody has a reason to go looking for it.
    fade grid to 1

    erase t_scale trees t_state

How sure are we? That good (@draw st1@) intentions can't stand in for missing knowledge is consensus.

B: How much it matters next (@draw st2@) to incentives, and whether it argues for a smaller state or one that lear (@highlight st2 effect underline@)ns faster, is contested.

    quiz "A health ministry ships the same yearly medicine order to every clinic. Some run out, others throw stock away. The officials are honest. What failed?"
        right: Dispersed knowledge: each clinic knows its own patients; the capital sees only averages.
        wrong: Who actually knows how many patients a clinic sees each month? Here, dispersed knowledge.
        * Incentives: someone gains from the waste
        + Dispersed knowledge: each clinic's needs are local
        * Unknowable knowledge: nobody could know the needs

    quiz "After 1973 US productivity growth slowed, but the central bank kept judging capacity by the old trend. If it was honest, what knowledge was missing?" voice b
        right: Changing: the old trend had been true, but it went stale, and the slowdown only showed in later data. Some argue policy was also simply wrong.
        wrong: Was the trend ever right? It was, until the economy changed. Here, changing knowledge.
        * Tacit
        + Changing: true once, stale by now
        * Dispersed

    quiz "A city's honest planners keep drawing bus routes that few people ride. Which reform targets a knowledge failure?"
        right: Try and count: it tells the planners something they didn't know. Pay and audits fix wanting, which was never the problem.
        wrong: Which option tells the planners something they don't already know? Here, try new routes and count the riders.
        * Pay the planners more
        * Audit them for bribes
        + Try new routes on a few lines first and count the riders

So why do governments fail even when they mean well? Because meaning well settles the wanting, and the hard part is the knowing.
    highlight c_know effect circle

B: And when they don't mean well, that's a different failure, with a different cure.
    highlight c_inc effect circle

